    "summary": "Monster Truckin' → The Other One jam and soaring The Other One",
    "blurb": "The second set shines with a monster Truckin' → The Other One jam that expands into a soaring The Other One, plus an inspired Playin' improvisation highlighted by Keith's Fender Rhodes. Jerry’s melodic leads and Bill’s crisp drumming keep the energy high, while Morning Dew closes the night on a poignant note.",
    "review": "June 29, 1973 at the Universal Amphitheatre captures the Dead in peak form during the spring‑summer tour. After a solid first set (albeit with portions missing from this source), the band launches into an electrifying second set that starts with Mississippi Half-Step Uptown Toodeloo and quickly builds momentum. The centerpiece is the Truckin' → The Other One jam – a monster stretch where Jerry melts the familiar Truckin' theme into fresh, exploratory territory before launching into a soaring rendition of The Other One. Playin' receives an inventive treatment as Keith adds tasteful Fender Rhodes texture, giving the jam a jazzy undercurrent while Bob supplies Wes‑Montgomery‑style chordal support and strong vocals. The set flows tightly through He’s Gone, Truckin', The Other One, and culminates with an emotionally resonant Morning Dew before the band wraps up with classic rockers Around & Around and Casey Jones. Throughout, Bill Kreutzmann’s drums are clear and well‑balanced in the mix, providing a steady pulse that lets each improvisational turn breathe.",
